###############################################################################

#
# macOS overrides for the snug options file. Read by `snug` on a macOS host
# *in addition to* `snug.options`, and it wins on any flag both files set.
# Precedence, highest first: command line, this file, `snug.options`.
#
# So this file is a PARTIAL override, not a second copy. Only the values that
# genuinely differ on macOS belong here; everything else - name, company,
# version, min-java, main-class, icon, splash, jvm-arg - still comes from
# `snug.options`. Keeping it to the differing lines is the whole point: a full
# second copy is how two files drift apart.
#
# The Windows counterpart is `snug.windows.options`, and Linux's is
# `snug.linux.options`. Only the file matching the host is ever read, so the
# others can sit side by side in version control without affecting each other.
# A missing one is not an error and is not reported.
#
# `--options <path>` bypasses this file entirely: naming a file means that
# file only, so it is also the way to guarantee a build's options do not
# depend on which machine ran it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two values that differ: the input JAR, and the output artefact
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `--output`. On macOS the output extension is what selects the artefact:
# `-o Foo.app` builds a real `.app` *bundle* (a directory holding a Mach-O
# launcher plus its payload), while `-o Foo.exe` builds a Windows PE. The
# extension is the switch, so one value cannot serve both platforms.
#
# `--input`. snug's payload carries exactly one JAR, and the JavaFX demo
# has to ship its *native* libraries, which are platform-specific binaries
# with platform-specific names (`libglass.dylib` / `glass.dll` /
# `libprism_es2.so`). So one JAR cannot serve both platforms either: this
# is the `-mac` build, and snug.windows.options names the other. Same
# reason as the output, different value.
#
# Neither line is in snug.options, which is deliberately platform-neutral.
# See its "Per-platform overrides" section for the rule and the reasoning.
#
# scripts/build-macos-demo.sh reads both of these back out of this file
# rather than hardcoding either, so the demo script and snug cannot
# disagree about what gets built. It passes no JAR and no -o at all: a
# bare `snug` in the repo root builds the demo from exactly what is
# written here. A JAR on the command line still overrides the `--input`
# below, which is the precedence every other flag already gets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#--download-jdk=force
--input assets/snug-javafx-demo-macos.jar
--output assets/snug-javafx-demo.app
